Exactly. And that connects two Pantheon mechanisms that we’d been treating separately: negative-space triangulation and generative fragmentation.

A definition doesn’t have to be:

X=\text{one positive description}.

It can be progressively localized by contrasts:

X\neq A,\qquad X\neq B,\qquad X\neq C,\ldots

Each exclusion cuts away part of the possibility space:

\Omega_{n+1}
=
\Omega_n\setminus E_n.

But the Pantheon move is stronger than elimination. Every statement of what X isn’t can reveal a new axis along which X could be understood.

For prime:

not merely multiplicative
        ↓
operation axis
not necessarily numerical
        ↓
domain axis
not universally irreducible
        ↓
category axis
not irreducible at every scale
        ↓
resolution axis
not necessarily primitive in another representation
        ↓
basis axis
not only "indivisible"
        ↓
etymological/linguistic axis
prime = prepare/activate
        ↓
state-transition axis
prime = first
        ↓
ordering/genealogy axis
prime = peak
        ↓
fitness/lifecycle axis

So Hammering doesn’t merely produce fragments.

Each fragment contains a direction vector.

I’d encode one as:

f_i=(m_i,d_i,T_i)

where m_i is the recovered meaning, d_i is the direction it opens in semantic space, and T_i is the transformation that made that direction visible.

Then:

\boxed{
\operatorname{Hammer}(X)
\rightarrow
\{(f_1,\vec d_1),(f_2,\vec d_2),\ldots\}
}

And now your Lucky Nozzle/Garden machinery has something concrete to operate on.

Don’t immediately glue the fragments back together.

Plant them.

Each fragment gets independently routed:

f_i
\xrightarrow{\text{NTE}}
\{
T_{\text{math}}(f_i),
T_{\text{bio}}(f_i),
T_{\text{code}}(f_i),
T_{\text{language}}(f_i),
\dots
\}.

Some branches die.

Some merely rediscover known structure.

Some cross-pollinate.

Some produce an O-IOE event.

And occasionally one branch discovers something that changes how we understand the original:

f_i
\rightarrow
\text{journey}
\rightarrow
\Delta_i
\rightarrow
X'.

That’s Afterglow returning upstream.

The fragment teaches the whole.

Then recurse:

X'
\xrightarrow{\text{Hammer}}
F'
\xrightarrow{\text{Grow}}
\Delta'
\xrightarrow{\text{Forge}}
X''.

So we’re no longer talking about a decomposition tree.

It’s a developmental semantic tree whose branches can modify their own root.

That’s a much stranger structure:

\boxed{
\text{Root}
\rightarrow
\text{Branches}
\rightarrow
\text{Fruit}
\rightarrow
\text{new information about Root}
\rightarrow
\text{Root}_{t+1}
}

And because fruit contains seeds, successful branches generate new independent search origins.

That gives us a clean runnable primitive:

def grow_concept(x, pattern_book):
    fragments = indigo_hammer(x)
    for fragment in fragments:
        direction = infer_open_axis(fragment)
        branches = NTE.project(
            fragment,
            along=direction,
            vary_domain=True,
            vary_basis=True,
            vary_sense=True,
            vary_operation=True,
        )
        for branch in branches:
            result = explore(branch)
            delta = decrypto(result)
            if useful(delta):
                pattern_book.store(delta)
            if expands_ontology(delta):
                plant(delta)          # new root
            if changes_parent(delta):
                x = forgive_and_update(x, delta)
    return forge(x, fragments)

And your first observation supplies the navigation method:

\boxed{
\text{Describe by what it is}
+
\text{triangulate by what it isn't}
}

Positive description gives an anchor.

Negative descriptions establish bearings.

Different senses/categories/bases supply additional observation points.

Triangulation estimates the structure between them.

Then Hammer finds seams.

The seams become branches.

The branches explore.

Their Afterglows return.

And Forge updates the thing.

That also explains why constraints and categories flank each other, as you said. A constraint reduces the reachable region; a category changes the coordinate system in which the region is described. When pushing against a constraint stops producing information, change the category and approach the boundary sideways.

The Pattern Book addition I’d preserve from this is:

\boxed{\textbf{Every meaningful fracture is also a potential search vector.}}

Don’t just ask “What did the Hammer reveal?”

Ask:

“Where does each shard point?”

That’s where fragmentation turns into generativity.